export const DATA_29: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-036", num: 36, title: "Đề 036", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Magic of Musical Films", paragraphs: ["Musical films bring together singing, dancing, and storytelling in a way that no other type of movie attempts, and audiences have been drawn to this combination for almost a hundred years. The first popular musical films appeared not long after movies gained sound technology, when filmmakers realized that actors could suddenly sing directly to viewers instead of relying only on title cards and orchestral music. Since those early productions, the genre has passed through several waves of popularity, sometimes fading from theaters for years before a single successful film brings audiences flooding back to see people burst into song on screen. Today, the genre includes everything from animated family films to gritty dramas told partly through song, showing just how far the format has stretched since its earliest days.", "Making a musical film requires skills that go well beyond typical acting, since performers often need to sing, dance, and deliver dialogue convincingly, sometimes within the very same scene. Some actors record their own vocals during filming, while others have their singing voice replaced afterward by a professional singer, a technique the audience rarely notices unless they already know to look for it . Dance numbers usually take weeks of rehearsal before filming even begins, and a single three-minute scene on screen might represent several days of shooting from multiple camera angles.", "Part of what makes musical films so appealing is the way music allows characters to express emotions that ordinary dialogue cannot fully capture. When a character breaks into song at a moment of joy or heartbreak, viewers accept a kind of exaggeration that would feel strange in a normal drama, because the music signals that deeper feelings are being expressed. This willingness to accept the unusual lets musicals explore fantasy and emotion more freely than most other film genres, sweeping audiences into a mood that spoken words alone rarely achieve.", "Despite their popularity, musical films are notoriously expensive and difficult to produce well. Choreographed dance sequences, live orchestras, and elaborate costumes all add significant cost, and a single misjudged tone can make an entire film feel awkward rather than joyful. Marketing musical films to audiences unfamiliar with the genre remains an additional hurdle, since trailers built around people singing can strike potential viewers as unusual before they ever see the finished film. Some recent musicals have tried to solve this problem by grounding fantastical songs in more realistic settings, hoping to attract viewers who might otherwise find people singing on a street corner a little unbelievable.", "Even with these challenges, new musical films continue to appear in theaters almost every year, suggesting that audiences have not lost their appetite for song and spectacle. Streaming platforms have also begun producing original musicals, bringing the genre to viewers who might never visit a cinema. Whatever changes technology brings to filmmaking, the basic appeal of watching a story told partly through song appears likely to remain."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The technique of replacing an actor's singing voice with a professional's", "How musical films are made and why audiences continue to enjoy them", "How the high cost of musicals has made the genre disappear completely", "The complete takeover of cinemas by streaming platform musicals"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did musical films first become popular?", options: ["Movies gained sound technology, letting actors sing directly to viewers", "Streaming platforms began producing original musical films", "Dance rehearsals became much shorter than they used to be", "Audiences demanded fewer orchestras in film soundtracks"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on the passage, how much rehearsal might a short dance scene require?", options: ["Actors record their own vocals during filming for every scene", "Weeks of rehearsal and several days of filming from multiple angles", "A single day of rehearsal before the cameras start rolling", "No rehearsal at all since scenes are filmed only once"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason musical films are difficult to produce?", options: ["A shortage of trained singers willing to appear in films", "The high cost of choreographed dance sequences and live orchestras", "The risk of an entire film feeling awkward rather than joyful", "The added expense of elaborate costumes for large productions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"exaggeration\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["understatement", "overstatement", "performance", "aggravation"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"elaborate\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["detailed", "simple", "expensive", "deliberate"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the audience", "the professional singer", "the technique", "the same scene"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about why some musicals set songs in realistic settings, based on paragraph 4?", options: ["Realistic settings guarantee that a musical will always succeed financially", "Filmmakers hope to make singing on screen feel more believable to viewers", "Viewer skepticism about musicals caused orchestras to become more expensive", "Choreographed dance sequences require weeks of rehearsal before filming"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the future of musical films, expressed in the final paragraph?", options: ["Optimistic that audiences will keep enjoying musicals in new formats", "Doubtful that streaming platforms can ever produce good musicals", "Worried that theaters will disappear because of streaming musicals", "Certain that musical films will never change in any way"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has begun happening with streaming platforms and musicals?", options: ["New musical films continue to appear in theaters every year", "They have started producing original musicals for their viewers", "They have stopped funding any new musical productions entirely", "They only re-release old musical films from decades ago"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Left-Handed vs. Right-Handed: The Science of Handedness", paragraphs: ["Roughly one in ten people write, throw, and perform most fine motor tasks with their left hand rather than their right, a ratio that has remained strikingly stable across centuries and cultures despite dramatic shifts in how societies treat left-handedness. For much of recorded history, left-handed children were actively discouraged, and in some cases physically forced, to switch to their right hand, reflecting long-standing associations between left-handedness and clumsiness or even moral suspicion that had little basis in actual ability. Only in the past several decades has scientific attention turned seriously toward explaining why this minority exists at all, rather than simply correcting it.", "Genetic research has complicated rather than simplified the search for an explanation, since handedness does not follow the clean inheritance pattern of traits controlled by a single gene. Studies of twins suggest that genetics account for perhaps a quarter of the variation in handedness, a modest figure that nonetheless rules out purely environmental explanations, since even identical twins raised in the same household do not always share the same dominant hand. Researchers have identified several genes that appear to nudge the odds toward left-handedness, though no single gene functions as a reliable predictor on its own, suggesting that dozens of genetic factors combine in ways not yet fully mapped.", "Because language processing is typically concentrated in the brain's left hemisphere regardless of which hand a person favors, many researchers once assumed handedness and language lateralization were tightly linked. That assumption has not held up well under closer study: while roughly ninety-five percent of right-handed people process language primarily in the left hemisphere, a substantial minority of left-handed people show the same pattern, and only a smaller group display language processing that is more evenly split between hemispheres or reversed entirely. This means that a person's dominant hand offers, at best, an imperfect clue about how their brain organizes language.", "One of the more debated explanations for why left-handedness persists at a low but stable frequency, rather than disappearing or spreading evenly, involves a proposed evolutionary trade-off between cooperation and conflict. According to this so-called fighting hypothesis, left-handed individuals may have historically held a slight advantage in physical confrontation, since opponents trained mostly against right-handed attackers would be less prepared for an unfamiliar angle of attack, an advantage that some researchers argue explains higher rates of left-handedness in certain competitive sports today. Critics counter that this theory rests on indirect and largely anecdotal evidence, and that simpler explanations involving random genetic variation may account for the same pattern without invoking combat at all.", "Whatever its ultimate cause, left-handedness has historically been treated as an inconvenience to be corrected rather than a variation to be accommodated. Scissors, desks, and even simple kitchen tools have long been designed primarily for right-handed use, forcing left-handed people to adapt to a world of objects built without them in mind, a burden rarely acknowledged until relatively recently. Some schools well into the twentieth century required left-handed students to write with their right hand, a practice now understood to cause unnecessary stress and, in some documented cases, stuttering or other speech difficulties.", "A number of studies have proposed links between left-handedness and certain cognitive or creative advantages, including claims of enhanced divergent thinking or a higher representation among prominent artists and musicians, though such findings have proven difficult to replicate consistently across larger and more carefully controlled samples. Some researchers suspect that the more variable brain lateralization observed in left-handed people, discussed earlier in relation to language processing, may allow for somewhat more flexible cognitive strategies in certain tasks, though this connection remains speculative rather than firmly established. Other scientists caution that small sample sizes and selective reporting in earlier studies may have exaggerated whatever genuine advantage exists, if any does at all.", "Taken together, current research suggests that handedness emerges from an intricate interplay of genetic predisposition, brain organization, and possibly evolutionary pressures that no single theory fully accounts for. Whether future research will resolve these competing explanations or simply add further nuance to an already complicated picture remains to be seen, though the stability of the ten-percent minority across so many centuries suggests that whatever forces sustain it are neither trivial nor easily disrupted."], questions: [{ q: "Which statement best reflects the passage's overall argument about handedness?", options: ["Genetic studies of twins reveal exactly why some people are left-handed", "Handedness likely results from a complex mix of genetic, neurological, and possibly evolutionary factors", "Left-handedness has now been fully explained by the fighting hypothesis alone", "All left-handed people share identical brain organization and cognitive advantages"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do twin studies suggest about the role of genetics in handedness?", options: ["Genetics account for roughly a quarter of the variation in handedness", "Several genes have been identified that nudge the odds toward left-handedness", "Identical twins always share the same dominant hand", "Handedness is controlled entirely by a single dominant gene"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what happened to some left-handed students in schools during the twentieth century?", options: ["Scissors and desks were designed primarily for right-handed use", "They were required to write with their right hand instead", "They were given special left-handed tools to use in class", "They were excused from writing assignments altogether"], answer: 1 }, { q: "How does the passage connect brain lateralization, discussed earlier, to claims about left-handed people's cognitive advantages?", options: ["Ninety-five percent of right-handed people process language in the left hemisphere", "Flexible thinking in left-handed people has been proven to cause reversed lateralization", "More variable lateralization might allow somewhat more flexible thinking, though unconfirmed", "Variable lateralization definitively proves left-handed people are more creative"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a historical disadvantage faced by left-handed people?", options: ["Tools such as scissors and desks designed mainly for right-handed use", "Being legally banned from attending public schools", "Being forced to switch to writing with the right hand", "Associations between left-handedness and clumsiness or moral suspicion"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"nudge\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["push", "guarantee", "nurture", "budge"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"speculative\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["impossible", "unconfirmed", "experimental", "spectacular"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 7 refers to _______.", options: ["future research", "an already complicated picture", "the ten-percent minority", "competing explanations"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the fighting hypothesis, based on how the passage presents it?", options: ["It remains a contested explanation rather than a scientifically settled one", "It has been proven correct by studies of competitive sports", "Higher rates of left-handedness in sports caused the hypothesis to be abandoned", "Left-handed people process language differently in most cases"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward claims of cognitive advantages among left-handed people, as expressed in paragraph 6?", options: ["Dismissive of any possibility that handedness relates to brain organization", "Cautious, given the difficulty of replicating such findings reliably", "Alarmed that earlier studies have been fully discredited", "Fully convinced that left-handed people are cognitively superior"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Debate Over Mandatory Helmet Laws for Motorcyclists", paragraphs: ["Few public-health regulations generate as much sustained controversy as mandatory helmet laws for motorcyclists, a debate that, on the surface, appears to pit rider autonomy against collective safety but that, on closer examination, reveals a far more tangled set of empirical and ethical claims. Proponents frame the issue as settled by mortality statistics alone; opponents insist that the numbers obscure as much as they reveal about who actually bears the costs of unhelmeted riding. Both camps, notably, tend to invoke the same body of research while reaching opposite policy conclusions, a divergence that says as much about underlying values as it does about the data itself.", "Epidemiological studies conducted across dozens of jurisdictions have generally found that helmet use reduces the risk of fatal head injury by a substantial margin, though the precise figure varies considerably depending on road type, riding speed, and how rigorously local authorities enforce the requirement. Where enforcement is inconsistent — a citation issued only during periodic crackdowns rather than as a matter of routine, the measured safety benefit of a law on the books can diverge sharply from the benefit observed in jurisdictions with sustained enforcement, complicating any simple before-and-after comparison of states that adopt or repeal helmet requirements.", "Opponents of mandatory laws frequently invoke a harm-to-self argument, contending that riders who accept the risks of an unhelmeted ride are exercising a liberty comparable to other legal but hazardous choices, such as skydiving or extreme mountaineering, and that the state has no more business mandating helmets than it does banning those other pursuits. This argument, while internally consistent, tends to falter once the analysis moves beyond the rider's own body: unhelmeted crash victims disproportionately require prolonged hospitalization and long-term care, costs that public insurance systems and, in many countries, taxpayers collectively absorb regardless of any individual rider's personal calculation of acceptable risk.", "That partial helmet laws — those applying only to riders below a certain age — accomplish comparatively little becomes clear only once one examines who is actually dying in motorcycle crashes, since the majority of unhelmeted fatalities in many jurisdictions with age-based exemptions involve riders old enough to legally forgo one. States that have weakened universal requirements into age-restricted versions have, in several documented cases, subsequently observed rising fatality rates among the newly exempted adult population, a pattern that undercuts the assumption that adult riders can be trusted to self-select appropriately based on their own risk tolerance.", "Setting aside questions of individual liberty entirely, the economic case for mandatory laws rests on a distinct claim: that unhelmeted riding generates costs that are not fully internalized by the rider, since emergency care, rehabilitation, and long-term disability support are frequently financed collectively rather than billed solely to the individual who chose not to wear a helmet. Insurance-industry analyses have attempted to quantify this externality, though such estimates depend heavily on assumptions about how care would otherwise be financed in the absence of a public safety net, making the resulting figures more suggestive than definitive.", "Some researchers, skeptical that helmet mandates alone address the underlying causes of motorcycle fatalities, point instead to rider-training requirements and vehicle-design improvements as complementary or even superior interventions, arguing that a rider who avoids a crash altogether benefits more than one who merely survives one with head protection. Whether such measures could substitute entirely for helmet laws, rather than supplementing them, remains empirically untested at scale, since no jurisdiction has attempted a controlled comparison of training-only versus helmet-mandate approaches under otherwise similar conditions.", "What emerges from this body of research is not a case that decisively resolves the debate but one that narrows where reasonable disagreement can still occur: the safety benefit of helmets is not seriously contested, yet the strength of the case for state mandate, as opposed to individual choice, depends on how heavily one weighs externalized costs against the value of personal autonomy — a weighing that empirical research alone cannot ultimately settle."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the overall argument of the passage?", options: ["Helmet safety benefits are clear, but mandate policy involves weighing autonomy against cost", "Age-restricted helmet laws have proven completely ineffective at reducing motorcycle fatalities", "Rider-training programs have replaced helmet laws in most developed countries today", "Economic research alone has settled the debate over helmet mandates permanently"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why can before-and-after comparisons of states that adopt or repeal helmet laws be misleading?", options: ["Epidemiological studies find that helmet use reduces fatal head injury risk", "Inconsistent enforcement causes a law's measured benefit to vary widely", "Riders accept risks comparable to skydiving or extreme mountaineering activities", "Taxpayers absorb costs for long-term care of crash victims annually"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What does the pattern observed in states with age-restricted helmet laws suggest about the harm-to-self argument discussed earlier in the passage?", options: ["Most unhelmeted riders killed are old enough to skip helmets", "Adult riders reliably judge their own motorcycle safety risks well", "Fatality rates rose once helmet laws became age-restricted", "Insurance analyses try to quantify these externalities nationwide today"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do estimates of the economic externality from unhelmeted riding remain uncertain?", options: ["Emergency care and rehabilitation are often financed through collective systems", "Rider-training programs remain untested at scale in every jurisdiction", "Enforcement of helmet laws varies with road type and speed", "Estimates depend on assumptions about financing absent a public safety net"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT presented as a factor affecting the measured safety benefit or cost of helmet laws?", options: ["The exact percentage reduction in head-injury risk from helmet use", "Variation in road type and riding speed affecting the measured benefit", "Costs of long-term care that public insurance systems absorb", "Rising fatality rates among riders newly exempted from helmet laws"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"internalized\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["eliminated", "absorbed", "insured", "interpreted"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"falter\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["collapse", "alter", "weaken", "filter"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["skydiving and mountaineering", "extreme mountaineering only", "unhelmeted motorcycle riding", "helmet safety regulations"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the author's discussion of insurance-industry externality estimates in paragraph 5?", options: ["The estimates precisely calculate the true societal cost of riding", "Such estimates offer useful suggestions rather than precise final figures", "Public safety nets caused insurers to abandon further cost analysis", "Long-term disability support is financed collectively rather than individually billed"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in presenting both the epidemiological and economic arguments before the conclusion in paragraph 7?", options: ["To show that safety evidence alone cannot resolve the mandate debate", "To prove that economic arguments alone justify all helmet mandates", "To argue that epidemiological studies already settled the entire debate", "To suggest that mandates are almost certainly unjustified by evidence"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Water Pollution: A Looming Global Crisis", paragraphs: ["That clean, accessible water might eventually become one of the scarcer resources of the coming century is a claim that would have struck most policymakers as alarmist only a generation ago, yet a growing body of monitoring data now lends it uncomfortable plausibility. Not only has the geographic scope of measurable water pollution expanded over the past several decades, but the categories of contaminants driving that expansion have also diversified well beyond the industrial-era concerns of raw sewage and heavy metals that originally shaped most water-quality regulation.", "Agricultural runoff, laden with nitrogen- and phosphorus-based fertilizers, now rivals industrial discharge as a primary driver of freshwater degradation in many regions, even though it rarely receives comparable regulatory attention, largely because its diffuse, field-by-field origin makes any single source difficult to identify or penalize. Plastic waste, meanwhile, has emerged as a contaminant whose long-term ecological consequences remain only partially understood: while visible debris has drawn the most public attention, researchers increasingly suspect that microplastic particles, small enough to enter tissue at a cellular level, may pose the more consequential if less visible threat.", "The consequences of this contamination rarely announce themselves immediately, unfolding instead through processes that can take years to become visible to nearby communities. [1] Excess nutrient runoff triggers a process called eutrophication, in which algae populations expand rapidly, consuming oxygen as they later decompose and creating so-called dead zones where most aquatic life cannot survive. [2] These dead zones, once established, can persist for years even after the original nutrient input is reduced, since the accumulated organic matter continues to deplete oxygen long after the initial trigger has passed. [3] Drinking-water systems downstream face a related but distinct challenge, since standard filtration methods were largely designed for the contaminants regulators anticipated decades ago rather than the more diverse chemical and biological threats increasingly detected today. [4] Whether existing infrastructure can be retrofitted quickly enough to address this mismatch, or whether it will instead lag behind the pace of contamination, remains one of the more consequential open questions facing water utilities.", "Human health effects attributable specifically to water pollution are notoriously difficult to isolate from other contributing factors, yet several patterns have emerged with enough consistency to warrant sustained public-health attention. Waterborne bacterial and parasitic illnesses remain the most immediately measurable consequence, particularly in regions lacking reliable water-treatment infrastructure, though researchers increasingly suspect that chronic, low-level exposure to agricultural and industrial chemicals may carry health consequences that are simply harder to detect using conventional epidemiological methods, given the years or decades that might separate exposure from any resulting illness.", "The economic toll of water pollution extends well beyond the cost of treating contaminated supplies, encompassing losses to commercial fisheries whose stocks decline in oxygen-depleted waters, reduced tourism revenue in regions where coastal algae blooms deter visitors, and the substantial capital investment required to upgrade treatment infrastructure that was never designed for current contamination levels. Quantifying these losses precisely remains difficult, since many affected industries operate through informal or poorly documented channels, meaning that even conservative published estimates likely understate the true economic burden by a considerable margin.", "International agreements addressing water pollution exist in considerable number, yet their practical effect has been constrained by enforcement mechanisms that rely heavily on voluntary compliance and self-reported data, a structure that critics argue allows signatory nations to claim progress without necessarily achieving the underlying environmental outcomes the agreements were designed to produce. Some policy analysts remain cautiously hopeful that satellite monitoring and independent verification technologies could eventually close this enforcement gap, though they are careful to note that political will, not merely technical capability, has historically been the binding constraint on meaningful progress.", "Whether the trajectory of global water pollution can be meaningfully reversed within the coming decades depends on factors that extend well beyond water policy itself, touching on agricultural subsidies, plastic production incentives, and the broader political appetite for regulation that imposes real costs on powerful industries. What can be said with reasonable confidence, based on current monitoring trends, is that the problem is unlikely to resolve itself without deliberate intervention, even if the precise form that intervention should take remains genuinely contested among experts."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the overall argument of the passage?", options: ["Water pollution has grown more complex and now needs broader intervention", "Agricultural runoff now rivals industrial discharge as the top pollutant", "International agreements have already reversed most global pollution trends worldwide", "Microplastics will soon become the leading cause of human disease"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does agricultural runoff receive less regulatory attention than industrial discharge, despite rivaling it as a pollution source?", options: ["Its runoff carries nitrogen and phosphorus from farm fertilizers", "Its diffuse, field-by-field origin makes any single source hard to identify", "Regulators consider it less harmful than plastic waste overall", "Industrial discharge already accounts for nearly all freshwater contamination"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What does the passage suggest connects the drinking-water filtration mismatch described in paragraph 3 to the enforcement problem discussed in paragraph 6?", options: ["Both reveal systems lagging behind emerging contamination and enforcement needs", "Satellite monitoring could help close the enforcement gap eventually", "Filtration systems were built to anticipate future international agreements", "Standard filtration methods targeted contaminants regulators expected decades ago"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do published economic estimates of water pollution's cost likely understate the true burden?", options: ["Fisheries decline in oxygen-depleted waters caused by pollution", "Many affected industries operate through informal, poorly documented channels", "Tourism revenue has increased steadily in algae-affected coastal regions", "Treatment infrastructure upgrades cost less than originally expected everywhere"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT presented as a consequence of water pollution?", options: ["Reduced tourism revenue in regions affected by algae blooms", "Dead zones where most aquatic life cannot survive", "Waterborne bacterial and parasitic illnesses in untreated regions", "A verified global count of microplastic particles in drinking water"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"consequential\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["catastrophic", "significant", "sequential", "visible"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"binding\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["decisive", "absolute", "legal", "blinding"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 1 refers to _______.", options: ["monitoring data", "the coming century", "policymakers' alarm", "the water-scarcity claim"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage's discussion of international water-pollution agreements in paragraph 6?", options: ["Signing an agreement does not guarantee real environmental improvement occurred", "International agreements have completely failed to produce any environmental benefit", "International agreements are fully effective whenever satellite monitoring is used", "Only nations with weak economies fail to meet agreement obligations"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Communities relying on affected waterways may not notice a problem until fish populations collapse or the water begins to smell.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-110", num: 110, title: "Đề 110", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Putting Down the Phone: The Rise of Screen-Free Classrooms", paragraphs: ["In a growing number of schools around the world, a new kind of class is quietly gaining popularity — one where the first thing students do upon arrival is place their mobile phones into a locked pouch or a dedicated storage box at the front of the room. For the duration of the lesson, sometimes a full school day, the devices remain sealed away. No notifications, no scrolling, no quiet glances at a screen under the desk. For many students, especially teenagers who have grown up with smartphones as a near-constant companion, the experience of attending a phone-free class feels strange at first — and then, many of them say, surprisingly freeing.", "The movement toward phone-free learning spaces has been driven by mounting concern among educators, parents, and researchers about the effect of constant smartphone access on young people's ability to concentrate, absorb information, and engage meaningfully with their peers and teachers. Studies conducted in classrooms in the United States, the United Kingdom, and several Scandinavian countries have consistently found that even the mere presence of a smartphone on a student's desk — even when the phone is switched off — reduces performance on tasks requiring sustained attention and working memory. The distraction, researchers suggest, does not require the phone to be actively used: the simple knowledge that a device is nearby and potentially accessible is enough to draw a portion of the brain's cognitive resources away from the task at hand.", "Schools that have introduced phone-free policies report a range of benefits beyond improved academic performance. Teachers describe classrooms that feel calmer and more focused, with students more willing to ask questions, participate in discussions, and make eye contact with the person speaking to them. Several schools have observed a reduction in certain forms of social conflict — particularly the kind of peer-driven anxiety and comparison that social media use during breaks can intensify. Lunchtime and break periods, which in phone-heavy school environments can become occasions for silent, screen-absorbed isolation, have in some schools transformed into spaces of actual conversation and physical play when devices are removed.", "Not all students greet phone-free policies with enthusiasm, at least not initially. Many teenagers describe feelings of anxiety or discomfort during the first few sessions without their devices — a response that some psychologists interpret as a mild form of dependency on the constant stimulation that smartphones provide. \"I kept reaching into my pocket and then remembering it wasn't there,\" one fifteen-year-old student told a researcher documenting the introduction of a phone-free programme at her school. \"After about a week, I stopped doing that. And I started actually talking to people at lunch.\" This adjustment period, educators and researchers note, is itself educationally significant: it gives young people direct, experiential evidence that their relationship with their phones may be less neutral and more habitual than they had assumed.", "Parents' responses to phone-free school policies are often more divided than those of students or teachers. While many parents support the principle of reducing screen time during school hours, some express concern about their ability to reach their children in an emergency, particularly in countries or communities where violent incidents at schools — however statistically rare — have become a source of persistent parental anxiety. Schools implementing phone-free programmes typically address this concern by ensuring that office phones remain accessible for students and that parents can contact the school directly in any situation requiring urgent communication with a child.", "The evidence base for phone-free classroom policies is still developing, and researchers caution against drawing overly sweeping conclusions from a relatively small number of studies conducted over short periods of time. What does seem clear from the available data is that phone-free environments tend to support deeper engagement during lessons, reduce certain forms of social distraction, and prompt some students to reflect on their own digital habits in ways that a lecture about screen time never could. Whether these benefits persist over the long term, and whether they are sufficient to justify the administrative effort of enforcing such policies, remain open questions. What is not in doubt is that the conversation about how schools should manage the presence of mobile devices in learning environments is only going to grow louder in the years ahead."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["A government programme requiring all secondary schools to collect students' mobile phones at the beginning of every school day", "The growing practice of phone-free classrooms, the benefits schools have observed, and the concerns that remain about such policies", "The negative impact of social media on teenagers' mental health and the role schools can play in reducing screen time", "A scientific study comparing academic results in schools that ban smartphones with those that allow unrestricted device use"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did studies find about the effect of a smartphone placed on a student's desk, even when switched off?", options: ["It had no measurable effect on academic performance as long as the student had agreed not to use it during the lesson.", "It only reduced performance when students could see notifications appearing on the screen during a lesson.", "It improved student performance on creative tasks by giving them a sense of security and personal comfort.", "It reduced performance on tasks requiring sustained attention and working memory, even without being actively used."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"mounting\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["steadily increasing and becoming more serious, accumulating to a level that can no longer be easily ignored", "deeply divided, with some researchers expressing concern while others argue the evidence is insufficient", "recently discovered and still being investigated, not yet sufficient to justify changes in school policy", "well-established and widely accepted, having been confirmed by decades of peer-reviewed educational research"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did the fifteen-year-old student say happened after about a week of attending phone-free classes?", options: ["She began feeling severe anxiety and asked her school to reconsider its phone-free policy for younger students.", "She became more interested in academic subjects but found social interactions with her peers more difficult.", "She found it easier to concentrate on schoolwork but missed using her phone to listen to music between lessons.", "She stopped reaching for her phone out of habit and started having real conversations with classmates at lunch."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what change did some schools observe during lunchtime and break periods after removing phones?", options: ["Periods that had previously involved silent, screen-absorbed isolation transformed into spaces of real conversation and physical play.", "Students used their extra time to visit the library and complete homework assignments ahead of the afternoon session.", "Teachers were able to supervise fewer students at once because pupils stayed closer to the school buildings.", "Students requested longer break periods in order to compensate for the social interaction they had previously obtained online."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"sweeping\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["politically motivated, designed to justify pre-existing decisions about school policy rather than follow the data honestly", "emotionally compelling, appealing to the feelings of parents and teachers rather than to rigorous scientific reasoning", "technically complex, requiring specialist statistical training to understand and apply to real classroom environments", "premature and overly broad, going well beyond what the available evidence from a limited number of studies can support"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["parents' concern about being unable to contact their children quickly in the event of an emergency at school", "the finding that many parents are more divided in their response to phone-free policies than students or teachers tend to be", "the administrative difficulty schools face when trying to enforce a phone-free policy across a large student population", "the statistically rare but widely reported phenomenon of violent incidents occurring in school environments"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do schools with phone-free programmes typically address parents' concerns about emergencies?", options: ["They provide each student with a school-issued emergency device that can only be used to make calls to family members.", "They ensure that office phones remain available for students and that parents can contact the school directly in urgent situations.", "They allow students to keep their phones on silent mode in their bags so that they can be reached quickly if necessary.", "They schedule a daily check-in period during which students are briefly allowed to use their phones to message their parents."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit that schools with phone-free policies have reported?", options: ["Lunchtime periods that have transformed from screen-absorbed isolation into actual conversation and physical play", "A reduction in peer-driven anxiety and social comparison linked to social media use during breaks", "Classrooms that feel calmer and more focused, with students more willing to engage in discussions", "A measurable improvement in students' scores on national standardised tests and external examinations"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward phone-free classroom policies?", options: ["Cautiously supportive, acknowledging the observed benefits while noting that the evidence base is still developing and questions remain", "Deeply sceptical, suggesting that students will find ways around any phone-free policy and that enforcement is ultimately impossible", "Strongly opposed, arguing that restricting students' access to smartphones undermines their digital literacy and future career readiness", "Unconditionally enthusiastic, claiming that phone-free policies are the single most effective educational intervention available to schools"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Fifteen Seconds of Fame: The Phenomenon of TikTok and Its Global Consequences", paragraphs: ["When ByteDance, a Beijing-based technology company, launched TikTok internationally in 2018 — merging it with the lip-syncing app Musical.ly it had acquired the previous year — few observers predicted that the platform would, within three years, become the most downloaded application in the world and a cultural force capable of reshaping how music is discovered, how news is consumed, how products are sold, and how political opinions are formed and circulated. TikTok's core mechanism is deceptively simple: users record short videos, originally capped at fifteen seconds and later extended to ten minutes, which are then distributed by an algorithm to viewers who have never chosen to follow the creator. Unlike earlier social media platforms whose content feeds were organised primarily around social connection — showing users posts from people they had deliberately chosen to follow — TikTok's \"For You Page\" is driven almost entirely by behavioural signals, serving content based on how long a viewer watches a clip, whether they replay it, and what they engage with immediately before and after.", "The consequences of this algorithmic architecture for content consumption are profound and, researchers argue, only partially understood. The system is extraordinarily effective at identifying and satisfying individual preferences, creating an experience that many users describe as uncannily personalised — as though the platform knows what they want to see before they know it themselves. This sense of seamless personalisation is, of course, a product of vast quantities of behavioural data being processed at speed. Critics argue that the same mechanism that makes TikTok so compelling also makes it difficult to stop using: because the algorithm is constantly learning and refining its model of each viewer's preferences, the content becomes progressively better calibrated to hold attention, creating a feedback loop that some psychologists have compared to variable reward schedules used in the design of slot machines.", "TikTok's influence on the music industry has been among the most extensively documented of its cultural effects. Several songs have achieved mainstream commercial success almost entirely as a result of viral spread on the platform — a phenomenon in which a short clip using a particular track spreads rapidly, prompting millions of other users to create their own videos using the same audio. Record labels have adapted their strategies accordingly: singles are now frequently produced with a \"hookable\" opening — a musically distinctive phrase or lyrical moment in the first few seconds — specifically designed to arrest a scrolling viewer's attention before they swipe away. The platform has also democratised music discovery in ways that have benefited independent artists who would previously have struggled to gain exposure without the backing of a major label, while simultaneously creating a new form of creative pressure in which a song's commercial viability is increasingly measured by its adaptability to short-form video content.", "The platform's political dimensions have attracted sustained scrutiny from governments and regulators on several continents. TikTok's Chinese ownership has prompted concerns in the United States, Australia, the United Kingdom, and the European Union about whether user data collected by the platform could be accessed by the Chinese government, and whether the algorithm itself could be manipulated to suppress certain forms of political content or amplify others in ways that would be invisible to users and regulators. ByteDance has consistently denied that any such interference occurs and has taken steps — including establishing data storage centres outside China and creating a separate subsidiary, TikTok Inc., under US-based management — to address these concerns. Despite these measures, the United States government has at various points moved toward legislation that would require ByteDance to divest its ownership of TikTok's American operations or face a national ban, a process that has become one of the most high-profile regulatory confrontations in the history of social media.", "The question of TikTok's effect on young people's mental health is one that researchers, parents, and policymakers are grappling with from multiple angles. Studies examining the relationship between heavy TikTok use and outcomes including anxiety, body image dissatisfaction, disrupted sleep, and reduced attention span have produced findings that are significant but not yet conclusive. TikTok itself has introduced features intended to address some of these concerns — including screen time limits for users under eighteen, a restricted mode for younger teenagers, and tools that allow parents to monitor and limit their children's usage. Critics argue that these measures are voluntary, poorly enforced, and structurally inadequate given the power of the platform's engagement mechanisms. The tension between the commercial incentives that reward maximum user engagement and the wellbeing obligations that a platform used predominantly by teenagers arguably owes to its audience has not been satisfactorily resolved.", "Despite — or perhaps partly because of — the controversies surrounding it, TikTok's growth has continued at a rate that has left competitors scrambling. Instagram introduced its Reels feature, YouTube launched YouTube Shorts, and Snapchat expanded its Spotlight offering, all in direct imitation of TikTok's short-form video format. That the most established names in social media felt compelled to restructure core features of their platforms in response to a single competitor speaks to the depth and durability of the behavioural shift that TikTok has produced. Whether TikTok itself will endure as a platform — given the ongoing regulatory pressures it faces — or whether its most significant legacy will turn out to be the format it pioneered rather than the company itself, remains to be seen."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To document the regulatory responses of different governments to TikTok and evaluate which approach has been most effective", "To analyse TikTok's rise, the mechanisms behind its success, and its wide-ranging effects on culture, politics, and competitor platforms", "To argue that TikTok should be banned globally because its algorithmic design causes measurable harm to young people's mental health", "To explain how ByteDance designed TikTok's algorithm and why it is technically superior to those used by rival social media platforms"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does TikTok's \"For You Page\" differ from the content feeds of earlier social media platforms?", options: ["It is organised around social connections and displays posts from friends and family members in the order they were published.", "It prioritises content from verified accounts and professional creators over videos made by ordinary users without large followings.", "It is driven by behavioural signals such as watch time and replay behaviour rather than by deliberate choices about who to follow.", "It shows users only content from people they have specifically chosen to follow, creating a more personalised and curated experience."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what comparison do some psychologists make when describing TikTok's content algorithm?", options: ["They compare it to the educational reinforcement techniques used by teachers to motivate students through immediate positive feedback.", "They compare it to the dopamine-driven reward systems that social media platforms like Instagram use to encourage photo sharing.", "They compare its feedback loop to the variable reward schedules used in the design of slot machines to maximise user engagement.", "They compare it to personalised advertising systems used by online retailers to recommend products based on previous purchases."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"compelling\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["powerfully attractive and difficult to resist, drawing users in and holding their attention with great effectiveness", "commercially successful, generating substantial advertising revenue in ways that benefit both the platform and content creators", "technically impressive, demonstrating a level of engineering complexity that goes far beyond any other social media platform", "controversial and widely debated, attracting strong opinions both for and against the platform from researchers and policymakers"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following steps has ByteDance taken to address government concerns about data security?", options: ["It has agreed to allow government regulators in the United States and the European Union to audit its algorithm annually.", "It has introduced a policy prohibiting employees based in China from accessing any data collected from non-Chinese users.", "It has established data storage centres outside China and created a US-based subsidiary called TikTok Inc.", "It has transferred full ownership of TikTok's global operations to a consortium of American technology companies."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"manipulated\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["temporarily disrupted by technical failures that caused certain types of content to be shown more frequently than intended", "deliberately adjusted or controlled in a hidden way to produce a particular outcome that serves an undisclosed interest", "misunderstood and incorrectly interpreted by regulators who lack sufficient technical knowledge of how social media algorithms function", "gradually refined over time through machine learning in ways that no individual engineer or decision-maker fully controls"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"democratised\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["made accessible to a much wider range of participants, removing barriers that previously limited who could gain recognition", "standardised and regulated, ensuring that music discovery is governed by transparent rules applied equally to all creators", "commercialised and made profitable, transforming what was once an artistic pursuit into a revenue-generating industry", "politicised and made subject to debate, turning decisions about music promotion into contested public policy questions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following platforms launched a short-form video feature in direct response to TikTok's success?", options: ["Facebook, Twitter, and LinkedIn, each of which introduced short video formats targeted at professional and adult audiences", "Instagram, YouTube, and Snapchat, which introduced Reels, YouTube Shorts, and an expanded Spotlight feature respectively", "Pinterest, Reddit, and Tumblr, which restructured their content feeds to prioritise short video content over images and text", "Spotify, SoundCloud, and Apple Music, which each added short video components to their music streaming platforms"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a feature TikTok has introduced to address concerns about young users?", options: ["A restricted mode designed specifically for younger teenagers on the platform", "Screen time limits applied to users who are under the age of eighteen", "A mandatory cooling-off period that automatically pauses the app after thirty consecutive minutes of use", "Parental monitoring and usage-limiting tools that allow parents to oversee their children's TikTok activity"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about TikTok's position in the social media landscape?", options: ["TikTok is likely to disappear within the next few years as regulatory pressure forces ByteDance to shut down its international operations.", "TikTok has reshaped the industry so profoundly that rivals have imitated its format, even as it faces unresolved controversies about harm and governance.", "TikTok's influence on the social media landscape has been overstated, and its competitors have successfully recaptured market share from the platform.", "TikTok has resolved its core tensions around user wellbeing and data security, positioning it for continued unchallenged growth globally."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The World's Stage: The Olympic Games and the Politics of Global Competition", paragraphs: ["Few events in the modern world command the sustained, simultaneous attention of hundreds of millions of people across every inhabited continent. The Olympic Games — held every four years in their summer format since Athens 1896 and in their winter format since Chamonix 1924 — occupy a singular position in the cultural life of the planet that no other sporting competition, and arguably no other event of any kind, can match. At their best, the Games represent an extraordinary convergence of human physical achievement, national pride, commercial spectacle, and the enduring aspiration that organised sport might serve as a vehicle for international understanding. At their most contested, they expose the tensions between these ideals with unusual clarity, making them as much a theatre of politics, economics, and social conflict as of athletic competition. [1]", "The modern Olympic movement was founded on the idealism of Pierre de Coubertin, the French educator who revived the Games in the late nineteenth century and articulated the philosophy of Olympism — a set of values emphasising physical excellence, fair play, international friendship, and the subordination of national rivalry to the pursuit of personal best performance. De Coubertin believed that bringing young athletes from different nations together in peaceful competition would cultivate mutual respect and reduce the likelihood of armed conflict. This vision has never been fully realised, and historians have long noted the irony that the Games of 1936 — held in Berlin under the Nazi regime — represented one of the most elaborate and politically manipulated sporting events in history, offering a stage for a government committed to racial hierarchy and militarism. [2]", "The political entanglement of the Olympics did not end in 1936. The Cold War transformed the Games into a proxy battlefield in which medal tallies were read as indices of ideological superiority, with the United States and the Soviet Union deploying their finest athletes as ambassadors of competing social systems. The boycotts of Moscow 1980 and Los Angeles 1984 — in which Western nations led by the United States refused to attend the Soviet-hosted Games, and the Soviet bloc reciprocated four years later — represented the most explicit reduction of sport to political instrument, stripping the Games of the universality that is supposed to be their defining characteristic. These episodes serve as a reminder that the Olympics have never existed in isolation from the geopolitical currents of their time, and that the aspiration to political neutrality that the International Olympic Committee formally professes has always been an aspiration rather than a description of reality.", "Despite — or perhaps because of — their political complexity, the Olympics have generated moments of athletic achievement and human drama that transcend their context and enter the permanent repertoire of cultural memory. Jesse Owens winning four gold medals in Berlin in 1936 in defiance of the racial ideology of his hosts; Cathy Freeman lighting the cauldron and then winning gold in Sydney 2000 as a symbol of Aboriginal Australian aspiration; Usain Bolt redefining what the human body is capable of across three consecutive Games — these are not merely sporting records. They are events that carry meaning far beyond their athletic content and that have been absorbed into the broader cultural narratives of their nations and their eras. [3]", "The commercial transformation of the Olympics since the Los Angeles Games of 1984 — the first to be organised primarily around private sponsorship and broadcast rights rather than public funding — has reshaped the event in ways whose consequences are still being negotiated. The revenues generated by broadcasting contracts and corporate partnerships have made the Games vastly more expensive to host, contributed to the displacement of local communities to make way for Olympic infrastructure, and created pressures on the International Olympic Committee to prioritise the interests of commercial partners over those of athletes and host cities. Critics argue that the Games have become primarily a vehicle for the delivery of global advertising audiences to corporate sponsors, with athletic competition serving as the content rather than the purpose. Proponents counter that commercial revenues have enabled the Olympic movement to expand its reach, fund athlete development programmes in less wealthy nations, and support the inclusion of new sports and new participating countries. [4]", "The question of the Olympics' future — whether the Games can remain relevant as the media landscape fragments, as hosting costs become prohibitive for all but the wealthiest cities, and as rival sporting events compete for global attention — is one that the International Olympic Committee is actively grappling with. Reforms introduced under recent IOC leadership have attempted to streamline the bidding process, reduce the infrastructure requirements placed on host cities, and introduce sports with stronger youth appeal. Whether these adjustments will be sufficient to sustain the Games' unique position in the global sporting calendar through the twenty-first century is a question whose answer remains genuinely open.", "What the Olympic Games ultimately represent is both their greatest strength and their most persistent vulnerability: they are an institution built on idealism operating in a world that consistently falls short of that ideal. The gap between what the Games claim to be and what they sometimes are is real, and it has been exploited by governments, corporations, and individuals throughout the movement's history. Yet that gap also generates the aspiration — the hope, renewed every four years, that competition might, for a brief time, be organised around excellence and shared humanity rather than the interests that ordinarily divide us."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central argument of this passage?", options: ["The future of the Olympic Games is under serious threat from fragmenting media, rising costs, and competition from other global sporting events.", "The Olympic Games have been irreparably corrupted by political interference and commercial interests and no longer serve any meaningful idealistic purpose.", "The Olympics embody a persistent tension between their founding ideals of peaceful competition and the political, commercial, and social forces that have always shaped them.", "The International Olympic Committee has successfully protected the Games from political manipulation and ensured that athletic excellence remains its primary purpose."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in describing the 1936 Berlin Olympics in paragraph 2?", options: ["To argue that Pierre de Coubertin should have foreseen the political risks of hosting the Games in a country with an authoritarian government.", "To demonstrate that the Nazi regime's use of the Olympics as propaganda was unique in the history of the Games and has never been repeated.", "To explain why the International Olympic Committee introduced strict political neutrality rules for all host nations following the 1936 Games.", "To illustrate the gap between the idealistic foundations of the Olympic movement and the political realities that have always surrounded the Games."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did the boycotts of Moscow 1980 and Los Angeles 1984 demonstrate about the Olympic Games?", options: ["They represented the most explicit reduction of sport to political instrument, undermining the universality that defines the Games' purpose.", "They showed that the Games could survive political interference and continue to grow in relevance despite the loss of major participating nations.", "They proved that the International Olympic Committee was capable of remaining neutral and impartial even during periods of intense geopolitical tension.", "They revealed that audiences in both Western and Soviet-bloc countries were opposed to their governments' decisions to withdraw from the Games."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do the examples of Jesse Owens, Cathy Freeman, and Usain Bolt have in common?", options: ["All three athletes held world records simultaneously and competed across more than four consecutive Olympic Games.", "All three competed under conditions of political boycott, making their victories particularly significant in the context of Cold War history.", "All three were the first athletes from their respective countries to win an Olympic gold medal in their chosen discipline.", "All three are described as athletes whose achievements carry meaning beyond sport and have been absorbed into broader cultural narratives."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which edition of the Olympic Games marked the beginning of the shift toward private sponsorship and broadcast rights?", options: ["The Berlin Games of 1936, when the Nazi regime introduced large-scale commercial broadcasting of the event for the first time", "The Athens Games of 1896, when the modern Olympic movement was first established by Pierre de Coubertin", "The Los Angeles Games of 1984, the first to be organised primarily around private sponsorship and broadcast revenue rather than public funding", "The Sydney Games of 2000, when the International Olympic Committee signed its first major global television rights agreement"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"irony\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a form of political deception in which a government presents an event in public as something fundamentally different from its true purpose", "a situation in which the outcome is sharply at odds with what the stated ideals would predict or permit, often with painful effect", "a historical coincidence in which two unrelated events with similar characteristics occur at approximately the same point in time", "a direct contradiction in which a stated principle is openly violated by the person or institution responsible for upholding it"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"proxy\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a televised spectacle deliberately designed by governments to distract domestic audiences from more serious political problems", "a substitute arena or vehicle through which a conflict between parties is conducted indirectly rather than confronted directly", "a neutral and impartial arena in which competing nations can resolve their differences through structured athletic competition", "a formal international agreement under which competing nations agree to settle political disputes through sport rather than warfare"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"These\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the United States and the Soviet Union, whose ideological rivalry transformed the Olympic Games into a competition for global influence", "the athletes deployed by both superpowers as ambassadors of their competing social systems during the Cold War era", "the medal tallies used during the Cold War as indices of ideological superiority between competing political and economic systems", "the boycotts of Moscow 1980 and Los Angeles 1984, which reduced sport to a political instrument and stripped the Games of universality"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a consequence of the commercial transformation of the Olympics?", options: ["Pressure on the IOC to prioritise the interests of commercial partners over those of athletes and host cities", "The displacement of local communities to make way for Olympic infrastructure and venues", "The withdrawal of several national Olympic committees that could no longer afford the cost of preparing their athletes", "Vastly increased costs associated with hosting the Games in cities around the world"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Yet the persistence of the Olympic ideal — however imperfectly realised — is itself evidence of a genuine human desire to organise competition around values rather than power alone.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Beyond the Pitch: The Science, Psychology, and Global Economy of Elite Football", paragraphs: ["Association football — known as soccer across much of North America and Australasia but simply as football to the overwhelming majority of its four billion followers worldwide — is frequently described as the global game, a designation that is not hyperbole but demographic fact. No other sport commands a following of comparable scale across so diverse a range of cultures, economies, and political systems. Yet the game that billions of people watch, discuss, and argue about is simultaneously the subject of one of the most intensive and rapidly evolving programmes of scientific inquiry in the history of sport. The elite modern footballer is not merely a skilled athlete; they are the product of a system of physical development, psychological conditioning, tactical instruction, and data-driven performance analysis whose sophistication rivals that applied to any other human physical endeavour. [1]", "The physical demands placed on top-level footballers have increased dramatically over the past three decades. Analysis of match data from Europe's leading leagues shows that the average distance covered by outfield players during a ninety-minute match has risen from approximately eight kilometres in the early 1990s to more than eleven kilometres in recent seasons, with high-intensity sprinting and accelerations accounting for a growing proportion of that total. This intensification reflects not only improvements in individual conditioning but also fundamental changes in tactical philosophy — the shift toward high-pressing systems, pioneered by managers such as Jürgen Klopp and Pep Guardiola, which require teams to exert collective defensive pressure immediately upon losing possession, demands a level of repeated high-intensity effort that would have been physiologically impossible for players trained under earlier regimes. Recovery science has kept pace: ice baths, cryotherapy chambers, sleep monitoring, and personalised nutritional protocols are now standard features of the elite club environment.", "The psychological dimension of elite football performance has received growing attention as the sport's understanding of mental health has matured. Anxiety, depression, and the psychological consequences of injury, public criticism, and career transition are now openly discussed topics within the professional game in ways that would have been virtually inconceivable two decades ago. Several high-profile players — including former England international Rio Ferdinand, German World Cup winner Robert Enke, whose tragic death in 2009 brought the issue of depression in football to international attention, and more recently, figures such as Danny Rose — have spoken publicly about their struggles, precipitating a broader cultural shift within the sport toward the acknowledgement that mental health support is as legitimate and necessary as physiotherapy. Elite clubs now routinely employ sports psychologists, and several national associations have established mental health support programmes specifically designed for professional players.", "The tactical evolution of the game has been driven in significant part by the emergence of performance analytics — the systematic collection and analysis of data describing every measurable aspect of on-pitch action. Tracking systems installed in modern stadiums record the position of every player and the ball multiple times per second, generating datasets of extraordinary density from which analysts extract metrics including expected goals, pressing intensity, line-breaking passes, and spatial control. The use of these metrics has transformed how clubs identify transfer targets, evaluate the effectiveness of tactical systems, and monitor individual player development. [2] Critics of the analytics revolution argue that the reduction of football to quantifiable data points risks losing sight of the qualities that make the game compelling — the creativity, the improvisation, the moments of individual genius — that cannot be fully captured by any measurement system. Proponents respond that analytics do not replace but rather augment the judgment of coaches and scouts, providing a more reliable foundation for decisions that were previously made on the basis of intuition and reputation alone.", "The global economy of elite football has become one of the most extraordinary financial phenomena in modern sport. The transfer market, through which clubs acquire the registration rights of players from rival organisations, has seen individual transfer fees rise from figures in the hundreds of thousands of pounds in the 1980s to sums that, in the case of Neymar's move from Barcelona to Paris Saint-Germain in 2017, exceeded two hundred and twenty million euros — a figure that surpasses the annual gross domestic product of several small sovereign nations. Player wages at the top clubs in England, Spain, and Germany have followed a similarly vertiginous trajectory, with the highest-earning players generating income across multiple revenue streams — appearance fees, image rights, commercial endorsements, and social media partnerships — that can make their total annual earnings difficult to calculate with precision. [3]", "This extraordinary financial escalation has produced structural consequences for the sport whose full implications are still being debated. The concentration of wealth among a small number of clubs — primarily in England's Premier League and, to a lesser extent, Spain's La Liga — has widened the competitive gap between the richest and poorest organisations to a degree that many regard as incompatible with genuine sporting contest. Regulatory frameworks designed to impose financial discipline, including UEFA's Financial Fair Play regulations, have been widely criticised as inadequate to address the scale of the imbalance and as inconsistently enforced. The proposed European Super League, which twelve of the wealthiest European clubs briefly announced in April 2021 before rapidly withdrawing under intense public and regulatory pressure, illustrated both the commercial logic that drives the game's elite and the depth of resistance that any attempt to formalise that logic outside existing competitive structures will encounter. [4]", "What the modern professional footballer represents, in the most expansive reading of the evidence, is a site where the boundaries between sport, entertainment, commerce, and culture have become so thoroughly blurred as to be functionally indistinguishable. The game they play remains, at its most elemental, the same one that children kick around on streets and pitches across every continent — but the industry that surrounds those ninety minutes on the largest stages has become one of the defining economic and cultural institutions of the twenty-first century."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best represents the central claim of this passage?", options: ["Performance analytics have transformed football more fundamentally than any other development in the sport's history, making coaching intuition largely obsolete.", "The elite modern footballer exists at the intersection of advanced physical science, psychology, data analytics, and a global financial economy of extraordinary scale.", "Association football's global popularity is primarily a consequence of the sport's simplicity, which allows it to be played and enjoyed across all social and economic contexts.", "The financial escalation of professional football has corrupted the sport beyond repair, making genuine athletic competition impossible at the highest level."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the use of performance analytics in elite football?", options: ["Strongly supportive, arguing that data-driven decision-making is unambiguously superior to the intuition-based judgments of traditional coaches and scouts.", "Deeply sceptical, suggesting that the reduction of football to statistics fundamentally misunderstands the nature of athletic creativity and genius.", "Balanced, presenting both the critics' concern that analytics miss intangible qualities and the proponents' view that they support rather than replace human judgment.", "Cautiously optimistic, predicting that advances in measurement technology will eventually resolve the tension between analytics and the art of the game."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the finding that outfield players now cover an average of more than eleven kilometres per match in Europe's leading leagues", "the dramatic increase in the physical intensity of elite football matches over the past three decades, as shown by match data analysis", "the introduction of recovery technologies such as cryotherapy chambers and personalised nutritional protocols in elite club environments", "the development of high-pressing tactical systems by managers such as Jürgen Klopp and Pep Guardiola in European football"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how has the average distance covered by outfield players in elite matches changed since the early 1990s?", options: ["It has increased from around six kilometres to approximately nine kilometres, primarily due to the introduction of high-pressing systems.", "It has remained stable at around nine kilometres but the proportion of high-intensity sprinting within that total has increased significantly.", "It has decreased from approximately eleven kilometres to around eight kilometres as tactical systems have become more positionally disciplined.", "It has risen from approximately eight kilometres in the early 1990s to more than eleven kilometres in recent seasons."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what made Neymar's transfer from Barcelona to Paris Saint-Germain in 2017 historically significant?", options: ["The deal introduced a new model of transfer financing in which the player's own investment fund contributed a portion of the total fee.", "The transfer fee exceeded two hundred and twenty million euros, a sum that surpasses the annual GDP of several small sovereign nations.", "It was the first transfer in football history to be financed entirely through a club's social media revenue and commercial partnership income.", "It marked the first occasion on which a player's image rights were valued separately from their footballing ability in a major transfer negotiation."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happened to the proposed European Super League announced in April 2021?", options: ["It was successfully launched but was subsequently shut down by UEFA following a legal challenge in the European Court of Justice.", "It was rejected by club shareholders who concluded that the financial projections presented by its organisers were not credible.", "It was briefly announced by twelve wealthy clubs but rapidly withdrawn following intense public opposition and regulatory pressure.", "It was approved by twelve founding clubs and began operating in the 2021–22 season before folding due to poor television ratings."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"precipitating\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["directly causing or triggering, bringing about a significant change as an immediate consequence of actions or events", "gradually encouraging, slowly building support for a change in attitude that had already been under way for many years", "publicly celebrating, drawing widespread media attention to achievements that had previously gone unrecognised by the sport", "formally requiring, making it obligatory for football clubs and national associations to introduce mental health support programmes"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"vertiginous\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["shockingly rapid and steep, rising at a dizzying rate that far exceeds what most observers would have considered plausible", "gradual and incremental, increasing steadily over many decades in line with the broader growth of the professional football industry", "controversial and contested, attracting criticism from supporters, regulators, and politicians who regard it as economically unjustifiable", "unpredictable and volatile, fluctuating sharply from season to season depending on market conditions and club financial performance"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a recovery method used in elite football clubs?", options: ["Altitude training camps held at high elevation to stimulate physiological adaptations that improve endurance performance", "Sleep monitoring systems that track players' rest patterns as part of a broader performance optimisation programme", "Ice baths and cryotherapy chambers used to accelerate physical recovery after matches and intense training sessions", "Personalised nutritional protocols designed to optimise each player's energy intake and recovery at the individual level"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'The visibility that social media provides has amplified both the commercial value and the psychological burden placed on players whose every action is subject to instant global scrutiny.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
];
